   "text": {
    "L1": "Mixtures are separated to remove unwanted parts or get useful parts.",
    "L2": "There is no single method for every mixture. Choose the method that matches the difference between the parts.",
    "L3": "Handpicking uses visible differences such as colour, shape or size. Threshing beats or crushes stalks to free the grain.",
    "L4": "Winnowing lets a mixture fall in moving air. The lighter husk blows farther, while the heavier grain falls nearby.",
    "L5": "Sieving uses particle size. Fine flour passes through, while larger pebbles or bran stay in the sieve.",
    "L6": "For rice, husk and small stones, winnow the husk first, then handpick the stones. Check what remains and sieve fine dust if needed.",
    "L7": "Different differences can require different methods in turn. The useful part is left clean.",
    "L8": "Now choose the method for removing small stones from a plate of dal."
   },
